50-54). Yet Ficino himself in a letter of the 1480s derides not only alchemy
but the very process of transmutation to which he appealed in the abovemen-
tioned passage of De vita. Writing to Jacopo Antiquari on the subject of bring-
ing back the Golden Age, he says in an elaborate conceit, "In the same measure
in which opinion deceives and fortune frustrates those vain men popularly called
Alchemists every time they labor to turn base metals into gold, in the same
measure the project of those men succeeds according to their wishes who as
far as possible subject concupiscence, irascibility, and action to contemplation -
so much so that they achieve in place of the rest of the metals gold, that is,
