the commentary as a literary form allowed him to say things which he believed
but which he could not as a Christian, and latterly as a priest, have said in
propria persona. But the authorities saw through the smoke-screen and under-
stood the voice to be his own.
Ficino was so sincerely enamored of daemons as to take this risk, I conjec-
