Finally, the third monster is to stay awake too often for much of the night,
especially after dinner, with the result that you are forced to sleep even after
sunrise. Since many scholars err in this and are deceived, therefore I will ex-
plain further how much it hurts the intelligence and I will give seven main
reasons. The first reason is drawn from the heavens themselves, the second
from the elements, the third from the humors, the fourth from the order of
things, the fifth from the nature of the stomach, the sixth from the spirits, and
the seventh from the phantasy.
First of all, three planets, as we said above [I. 11, especially favor reflection
and eloquence: the Sun, Venus, and Mercury. Since they run almost in step
together, at the approach of night these planets flee from us, but with day ap-
proaching or now rising they arise again and revisit us. But after sunrise, they
are immediately thrust into the twelfth house of heaven, which is assigned by
astronomers to prison and darknes5 Therefore it is not people who rise either
at night, when they flee from us, or during the day after sunrise, when they
enter the house of prison and darkness, who explore things most acutely and
write and compose their findings most eloquently, but those people alone who,
when they are either about to rise or now rising, themselves rise up with them
to reflect and write.
The second reason, which is from the elements, is this: at sunrise the air
is stirred, rarefied, and clear; but just the opposite happens at sunset. The blood
and the spirits are compelled by necessity to imitate the motion and quality
of the air because it surrounds them and is similar in n a t ~ r e . ~
