On Excessive Sleeplessness.
Chap. XXIV
1t often happens to melancholics, especially to those who are learned, that
when their brain has been dried out with long bouts of sleeplessness, they
become thin. Since nothing increases the evils of black bile more than long
bouts of sleeplessness, every effort must be made against this great evil. There-
fore they should eat lettuce after other foods, together with a moderate amount
of bread and a little saffron, drink pure wine after the lettuce and not study
late. Whenever they are ready to sleep, let them take the following confection,
in which there are two ounces of white poppy seed, one ounce of lettuce seed,
one-half dram apiece of amomum and saffron, and six ounces of sugar. Dilute
