ly in between. Nevertheless, we will never try to root out the natural condition
of the body lest we root out the very life. Moreover, while we are striving to
nourish a slender man with denser foods, it is of advantage to remember that
when we fear that the blood is thin and meanwhile the stomach is not very
strong by nature, the patient's blood must ascend to thickness by smaller steps;
and in the meantime his stomach must be fomented and his sleep prolonged;
bodily exercise must be increased as far as possible and that of the mind must
be lessened, because it is very often harmful to many people. But if he cannot
bear foods which are too viscous and hard or very cold, we will eventually at-
